!<><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><>!
!
!-------PRF & 2-methyl-tetrahydrofuran Detailed High Temperature Mechanism------
!
! PRF mechanism from Chaos, Kazakov, Zhao, and Dryer; Princeton University, 2006.
! MTHF mechanism from Bruycker et al. 2017 Combustion and Flame 176: 409-428. 
!
!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!! IMPORTANT !!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!
!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!! IMPORTANT !!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!
!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!! IMPORTANT !!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!
!
!  HOW TO USE THIS MECHANISM:
!
! (1) This mechanism contains several pressure dependent reactions which
!     cannot be expressed with CHEMKIN-compatible falloff expressions.  While
!     pressure dependence of most of these rates in the C2-C3 portion of this 
!     mechanism is likely to have a very small effect on the observables of
!     (anticipated) interest; the user must be aware of this fact. For
!     consistency, however,
!
!     PLEASE CHOOSE THE REACTION RATE CORRESPONDING TO THE APPLIED 
!     PRESSURE RANGE.
!
!
! (2) Due to limitations of CHEMKIN-II format (specifically, an inability to
!     implement temperature-dependent collision efficiencies in falloff
!     reactions) and the lack of fundamental understanding of the mixing rules
!     for the falloff reactions with the bath gases that have different
!     broadening factors, the present implementation represents a compromise
!     (approximate) formulation.  As a consequence,
!
!     PRIOR TO ITS USE IN THE CALCULATIONS, THIS FILE HAS TO BE MODIFIED.
!     DEPENDING ON WHAT BATH GAS (DILUTANT) IS MOST ABUNDANT IN YOUR SYSTEM
!     (THE PRESENT CHOICES ARE N2, AR, OR HE),  YOU  SHOULD UNCOMMENT THE
!     CORRESPONDING BLOCK FOR THE REACTION H+O2(+M)=HO2(+M), AND COMMENT THE
!     BLOCK FOR OTHER DILUTANT(S).  AS GIVEN, THE MAIN DILUTANT IS SET TO BE N2.
!! !
!<><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><><>!

ELEMENTS
C H O N AR HE
END

SPECIES
